precept UnitOfMeasureReference

# A stateless data-only precept governing a single unit-of-measure definition.
# Each record describes one unit — its identifier, the dimension family it
# belongs to, the base unit of that family, and the conversion factor needed
# to express one of this unit in terms of the base.
#
# Units of measure are reference data — workflow precepts that carry physical
# quantities (shipments, dosages, lab results) use UCUM-aligned unit codes
# directly through the `quantity` and `price` types. This precept exists for
# host applications that need to govern a custom unit catalog alongside the
# built-in UCUM tier-1 units the runtime already knows.
#
# UCUM (Unified Code for Units of Measure) is the reference standard. The
# Dimension choice mirrors UCUM's seven base dimensions plus the dimensionless
# "count" family used for discrete things (each, dozen, gross), and a few
# named compound dimensions commonly governed alongside the base ones.
#
# ConversionFactor expresses how many of the base unit equal one of this unit:
#   gram (base = gram)        — ConversionFactor = 1
#   kilogram (base = gram)    — ConversionFactor = 1000
#   pound (base = gram)       — ConversionFactor = 453.59237
#   ounce (base = gram)       — ConversionFactor = 28.349523125
#
# IsBaseUnit marks the canonical unit of its dimension (the one with factor 1).

# Identity
field Code as string optional maxlength 32 writable
field DisplayName as string optional maxlength 100 writable
field Symbol as string optional maxlength 16 writable

# Dimension classification — what the unit measures.
field Dimension as choice of string("Length","Mass","Time","ElectricCurrent","Temperature","Substance","Luminosity","Count","Area","Volume","Speed","Acceleration","Force","Energy","Power","Pressure","Frequency","Angle") default "Count" writable

# BaseUnitCode references the canonical unit of the dimension. For SI Mass
# that's 'g' (gram); for SI Length that's 'm' (metre). When IsBaseUnit is true
# BaseUnitCode and Code refer to the same unit.
field BaseUnitCode as string optional maxlength 32 writable

# ConversionFactor — how many of the base unit equal one of this unit.
field ConversionFactor as decimal default 1 positive maxplaces 12 writable

field IsBaseUnit as boolean default false writable

# UcumExpression is the formal UCUM syntax for this unit (e.g. 'kg', 'm/s2').
# The runtime accepts UCUM-compatible expressions directly in quantity types;
# this field exists so a host UI can render the canonical UCUM string.
field UcumExpression as string optional maxlength 64 writable

# System classifies which measurement system this unit belongs to.
#   SI            — International System of Units (kg, m, s, ...)
#   USCustomary   — US customary (lb, ft, gal, ...)
#   Imperial      — British Imperial (stone, mile, pint, ...)
#   CGS           — centimetre-gram-second (cm, g, dyne, erg, ...)
#   UCUM          — UCUM-only convention (e.g. unified codes)
#   Custom        — application-defined (case, pallet, batch)
field System as choice of string("SI","USCustomary","Imperial","CGS","UCUM","Custom") default "UCUM" writable

field Active as boolean default true writable
field Notes as string optional maxlength 500 writable

rule ConversionFactor == 1 when IsBaseUnit because "A base unit must have a conversion factor of exactly 1 — the base unit is the reference point of its dimension and converts to itself"
rule ConversionFactor != 1 when IsBaseUnit == false because "A non-base unit must have a conversion factor different from 1 — a factor of 1 would make it indistinguishable from the base unit of the same dimension"
